meta {
  name: Upload Product Image
  type: http
  seq: 12
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/products/{{productId}}/images
  body: multipartForm
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
}

body:multipart-form {
  file: @file(/absolute/path/to/product-photo.png)
}

vars:pre-request {
  productId: 4d955c78-36be-4089-84ab-f93cabcf818e
}

docs {
  Admin-only. The API receives the file and uploads it to S3 itself — the client never talks to
  S3 directly.

  Key format: product-images/{productId}/{uuid}.{ext}
  Allowed types: image/jpeg, image/png, image/heic, image/webp
  Max size: 4 MB (Lambda's event payload is capped at 6 MB and the binary arrives base64-encoded)

  The response returns the final image URL. The URL is appended to products.images asynchronously
  by the processProductImage SQS consumer, so it may take a moment to show up on
  GET /products/sku/{sku}.

  Ordering is not guaranteed when several uploads for the same product are in flight — standard
  SQS queues do not preserve delivery order. Use Update Product with a full `images` array to fix
  the order and choose the main image (the first element).
}
